; LIMIT 04: arithmetic wraps silently, and nothing reports that it did.
;
; add, sub, mul, neg, and div wrap on overflow (spec 3, 6.1), which is
; right for a hash but wrong for a calculator, whose user asked for the
; true value. L0 has no checked arithmetic and no bitwise operations, so the
; usual one-line tests (the sign bits of a ^ r and b ^ r) cannot be written
; either. calc detects overflow by case analysis on signs, with the
; -9223372036854775808 * -1 and / -1 cases handled apart:
;
;   calc.l0   overflow, checked_add, checked_sub, checked_mul, checked_neg,
;             checked_div, checked_mod: 38 lines
;   calc_test.l0  a property test of checked_mul against a division-based
;             bound (37 lines), and nine edge-case assertions, to trust
;             those 38 lines
;
; A number too large to be a literal is the same problem in the lexer, which
; checks v > (max - d) / 10 before each digit.
;
; The smallest program: it prints -9223372036854775808, exit status 0, and
; no program can tell from the result that anything went wrong.

(def main () int
  (let w (write_line (digits (add 9223372036854775807 1)))
    0))
